  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Show that there are exactly two Pythagorean triples of the form $(9,b,c)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(9,40,41)$ and $(9,12,15)$.\n\nThe unequal positive factor pairs of $81$ are $(1,81)$ and $(3,27)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}9^2+b^2&=c^2\\\\c^2-b^2&=81\\\\(c-b)(c+b)&=81\\end{align*}$$Since $c>b>0$, the factors are positive and unequal. The positive factor pairs of $81$ are $(1,81)$, $(3,27)$ and $(9,9)$; the last gives $b=0$ and is excluded.\n\nFor $(c-b,c+b)=(1,81)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}c-b&=1\\\\c+b&=81\\\\2c&=82\\\\c&=41\\\\b&=40\\end{align*}$$For $(c-b,c+b)=(3,27)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}c-b&=3\\\\c+b&=27\\\\2c&=30\\\\c&=15\\\\b&=12\\end{align*}$$The checks $9^2+40^2=1681=41^2$ and $9^2+12^2=225=15^2$ confirm both triples. All possible factor pairs have been considered, so there are exactly two.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 132
  },
